      "writtenOn": "2020-07-15T20:36:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This looks like it should strengthen the sync flag. Does this patch solve the issue?\n\nHow does it behave if waker is CFS? The normal CFS sync behavior is that the wakee is put on the rq of the same cpu so when the waker yields, the wakee gets the CPU. I think this will also be the behavior with this patch if the waker is RT and the wakee is RT with the same or lower prio (where \"lower prio\" means a higher priority number).\n\nBut, if a CFS task wakes an RT task with this patch, the waker will be preempted and then will resume later (most likely for a very short duration), so it incurs a extra context switches. Should we consider suppressing sync for this case?\n\n",
      "revId": "f71079542c53f3ada8176d9980dacb44200a623a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7ca210c1_58910061",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1688863
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-15T20:47:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With that in mind, I think it\u0027s fine to suppress sync in the \"CFS wakes RT\" case. Though, for my own knowledge, under what circumstances do we expected to see this? Are there any Android-specific paths leading to this behavior that I should be aware of?",
      "parentUuid": "841f8f2d_ee816d3c",
      "revId": "f71079542c53f3ada8176d9980dacb44200a623a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4e434b21_1e214f06",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-15T20:59:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The main Android use-case for sync is binder. So we get this case if the client is CFS and the server is RT (SCHED_FIFO). There are some HAL processes that use SCHED_FIFO (by setting the per-node min prio), so any time a CFS client interacts with those HALs we have this case. It might be that we don\u0027t care about the perf of those cases, but I\u0027m not sure.",
      "parentUuid": "7ca210c1_58910061",
      "revId": "f71079542c53f3ada8176d9980dacb44200a623a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
